<item>
 <title>A toast to Karl Otrok</title>
 <link>https://coveredinbees.org.archived.website/node/260</link>
 <description>&lt;p&gt;I&#039;ve watched three films about food recently; I&#039;ll write about them together at some point, continuing the &#039;3&#039; theme. Right now, I want to raise a glass to one man with a walk-on part in the Austrian film &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.we-feed-the-world.at/index.htm&quot;&gt;We Feed the World&lt;/a&gt;, currently available &lt;a href=&quot;http://video.google.com/videoplay?docid=-7738550412129841717&quot;&gt;via Google video&lt;/a&gt; and we join Karl Otrok in his four-wheel drive at about 39 minutes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Karl Otrok is - or, I suspect, was - director of Pioneer&#039;s Romania operations. &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pioneer_Hi-Bred&quot;&gt;Pioneer&lt;/a&gt; is a US-owned seed company, second only to Monsanto. They&#039;re owned by DuPont. Karl begins his tale in what appears to be full High Modern mode: contrasting the four-wheel-drive lifestyle of a Pioneer director to the horse-drawn power of much Romanian farming. But while he begins as ambassador for Pioneer - and clings to his company loyalty throughout - we witness a rapid landslide of his veneer of self-belief.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://coveredinbees.org.archived.website/node/260&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;read more&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
